### Method Explanation
The problem asks for the minimum total cost to sort a permutation $P$ using adjacent swaps, where swapping elements at indices $i$ and $i+1$ costs $i$. Because the cost depends strictly on the left index of the swap, we want to perform all unavoidable swaps (inversions) as far to the left as possible. 

For any two inverted elements $A > B$ (where $A$ is initially to the left of $B$), we can reduce the index at which they swap if we can first shift other elements out of the way. If we process the elements from largest to smallest ($N, N-1, \dots, 1$) and shift each to its correct final position, we guarantee that larger elements are pushed to the right, naturally reducing the index (and thus the cost) of all subsequent swaps involving smaller elements. This strategy effectively minimizes the left-side prefix length for every single swap.

When it is element $v$'s turn to be shifted into its correct position $v$, all elements strictly greater than $v$ have already been shifted to the far right. Therefore, the elements remaining in the first $v$ positions are precisely those $\le v$. We can find the current relative position $p$ of $v$ among them by using a Fenwick tree (Binary Indexed Tree) to count how many elements $\le v$ were initially placed at or before $v$'s original starting position. Moving $v$ to the end of this prefix of length $v$ requires swapping it with $v-p$ elements. The indices at which these swaps happen will be $p, p+1, \dots, v-1$, and the total cost incurred for element $v$ will simply be the sum of this arithmetic progression: $\frac{(p + v - 1)(v - p)}{2}$.

### Code Improvements
